# The commit message should ideally abide by the Conventional Commits
# specification.
# https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/

<type>([scope]): <subject>

# [body]

# Closes: #n
# Deprecated: 
# Breaking Change:

# Commit Types (required, select one)
# 
# build: Changes that affect the build system or external dependencies
# ci: Changes to CI configuration files and scripts
# chore: Other (minor) changes that do not modify /src
# feat: New feature
# fix: Bug fix
# docs: Documentation only changes
# perf: Code change that improves performance
# refactor: A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature
# test: Correct existing specifications or otherwise define them

# Scope (optional)
# Generally you should reference which feature in `/src/cases` are the set of
# changes you're committing is located.

# Subject (required)
# Concise explanation of change to commit.

# Body (optional)
# A more detailed explanation of what the changes.
#
# Don't explain *how* it's changed, it does not add any useful information that
# isn't already explained by the changes you're committing.
#
# Focus on *what* has changed and *why* they've been changed.

# Footer (optional)
# Additional context

# Additional Information:
#
# When committing a breaking change, add an exclamation mark ! just before the
# colon :
